THE COUNTRY IS ENDING AGAIN

Matt Goodwin, Permanent Emergency, and the Industrial Production of Political Alarm

A satirical examination of a political analyst who appears to have become his own research subject

⸻

Abstract

This paper investigates an unusual development in British political science.

Matt Goodwin spent a substantial academic career studying populism, political extremism, voter behaviour and the rhetorical techniques through which political movements convert social anxiety into political mobilisation.

He subsequently left academia, became a broadcaster and subscription-funded political commentator, stood as a parliamentary candidate for Reform UK, and began producing material containing titles such as:

“THEY THINK YOU’RE STUPID.”

“The EARTHQUAKE that could be about to erupt in British politics.”

“We’re running out of time.”

“The TRUTH they’re not telling you…”

and:

“Why an obscure local election should TERRIFY the Right…”

These are not parodies invented for this paper. They are titles used by Goodwin himself. 

This presents an intriguing methodological opportunity.

Rather than asking whether Goodwin remains an impartial political analyst, an increasingly difficult question given that he recently stood for Reform UK, this paper asks something simpler:

What actually happens if we treat his arguments with the seriousness their presentation requests?

The preliminary finding is unfortunate.

A considerable portion of the analytical machinery can be approximated by:

\text{Event}
\rightarrow
\text{Threat}
\rightarrow
\text{Betrayal}
\rightarrow
\text{Britain}
\rightarrow
\text{Capital letters}

with an optional subscription prompt appended afterwards.

The paper names this phenomenon the Goodwin Permanent Emergency Machine.

Its principal product is not policy.

Its principal product is alarm.

⸻

1. From Studying Populism to Becoming Extremely Convenient Research Material

Matt Goodwin is not an unintelligent man.

That would actually make this paper considerably less interesting.

He completed a doctorate, became a professor of political science, published extensively on the BNP, UKIP, Brexit and national populism, advised governments and institutions and developed an academic reputation around precisely the political phenomena discussed here. He left the University of Kent through voluntary severance in 2024. His present biography describes him as a writer, pollster, campaigner and adviser. 

Then something fascinating happened.

The zoologist climbed into the enclosure.

Goodwin stood as Reform UK’s candidate in the February 2026 Gorton and Denton parliamentary by-election. He later described himself as representing Reform alongside Danny Kruger during a Spectator debate. Reform’s own website currently republishes his commentary. 

There is nothing inherently improper about this.

People are allowed political beliefs.

Academics are allowed to become politicians.

Commentators are allowed to campaign.

But one should probably update the label on the jar.

Someone campaigning for Reform, standing for Reform and producing material distributed by Reform is no longer especially mysterious in his political orientation.

Calling this bias is almost unnecessarily complicated.

It is advocacy.

The more interesting question concerns the continued performance of analysis around it.

