2. The Goodwin Permanent Emergency Machine

Consider the screenshots accompanying this paper.

One informs readers that the Conservatives “betrayed Britain.”

Another announces the “ongoing destruction of the United Kingdom.”

Another asks followers to endorse deporting foreign nationals who facilitate forced child marriage.

Another treats an obscure council by-election as carrying grave implications for the entire British Right.

The basic rhetorical system is remarkably economical.

Stage One: Select an event

An election.

An immigration statistic.

A government document.

A crime.

A poll.

A policy announcement.

Stage Two: Increase voltage

The event is rarely simply interesting.

It is:

TERRIFYING.

An EARTHQUAKE.

A BETRAYAL.

Evidence that Britain is being DESTROYED.

Something THEY DON’T WANT YOU TO KNOW.

We are RUNNING OUT OF TIME.

Stage Three: Construct two groups

There is normally a virtuous public:

P = \text{ordinary / decent / patriotic / ignored people}

and an antagonist:

E = \text{elite / establishment / Labour / liberal / migrant / institutional actor}

Complex political systems are thereby compressed into something approximately the narrative complexity of Thomas the Tank Engine.

Good people.

Bad people.

Trouble on the railway.

