3. An Experimental Test: The Council By-Election That Was Asked to Carry Britain on Its Back

On 31 August 2026, Goodwin published:

“Why an obscure local election should TERRIFY the Right in British politics – and might tell us A LOT about the next general election”

The election concerned one council ward in Kent. 

A local election can certainly provide useful information.

Political scientists study them constantly.

The difficulty is the inferential distance between:

\text{one ward}

and

\text{national political destiny}.

Any responsible analysis needs to consider candidate effects, turnout, local organisation, demographics, incumbency, sampling error, tactical behaviour, ward-specific issues and whether the observation generalises.

The Goodwin headline instead has already arrived at:

TERRIFY

before the reader has reached paragraph one.

This is political analysis performed in the style of a fire alarm.

⸻

4. The Democracy Problem

The screenshots reveal another interesting asymmetry.

Goodwin writes:

“Imagine looking at the ongoing destruction of the United Kingdom and then celebrating the Greens and Labour winning elections.”

Consider that sentence carefully.

The Greens and Labour winning elections is placed within a description of the “destruction of the United Kingdom.”

But elections are the mechanism through which citizens select political representation.

People voted.

Some voted incorrectly, apparently.

This creates an interesting model of democratic legitimacy:

\text{Right wins}
\rightarrow
\text{popular revolt}

while

\text{Left wins}
\rightarrow
\text{national destruction}.

Democracy therefore remains sacred right up until the electorate starts freelancing.

