5. A Particularly Useful Natural Experiment: Matt Goodwin Himself

Political commentators enjoy explaining electoral behaviour.

Fortunately, in February 2026 Goodwin provided researchers with the rare opportunity to observe what happens when the commentator himself becomes the candidate.

Before the Gorton and Denton by-election, Goodwin told supporters Reform had a “real chance” of winning and said that victory could bring down the prime minister and demonstrate that:

“if we can win here, we can win anywhere.” 

The electorate subsequently returned the following result:

\begin{array}{l|r}
\text{Hannah Spencer, Green} & 40.7\%\\
\text{Matt Goodwin, Reform} & 28.7\%\\
\text{Angeliki Stogia, Labour} & 25.4\%
\end{array}

Spencer beat Goodwin by 4,402 votes. 

This does not make Goodwin stupid.

It does provide a useful calibration exercise.

Prediction:

\text{Potential historic political earthquake}

Observation:

\text{Lost to plumber by twelve percentage points}.

Science progresses through falsification.

Satire occasionally gets lucky.

⸻

6. When Losing Becomes Additional Evidence That You Were Right

The truly impressive feature of permanent-emergency politics is its resistance to disconfirmation.

If your side wins:

The people are awakening.

If your side loses:

The system is broken.

If polls agree:

The silent majority has spoken.

If polls disagree:

The establishment refuses to listen.

If people vote differently:

Sectarianism.

This is an unusually powerful explanatory framework because almost no observable outcome can falsify it.

Six months after losing Gorton and Denton, Goodwin argued that voters in heavily Muslim areas had placed religious and clan loyalties ahead of Britain and accused the Greens of encouraging sectarian politics. 

Separately, allegations of illegal “family voting” arising from that election were investigated by Greater Manchester Police and examined by the returning officer. The council subsequently stated that the allegations were not substantiated and that the integrity of the election had not been affected. 

The distinction matters: that investigation does not disprove every broader argument Goodwin makes about sectarian voting.

But it provides an excellent illustration of why inflammatory political claims need evidence before the caps lock rather than afterwards.

