7. Rage, But With a Monthly Subscription

This is where the paper gets really interesting.

Calling Goodwin a man who “monetises rage” as a statement about his private motives would require knowing what is inside his head.

We do not.

Luckily, we don’t need to.

We can examine the economic system.

Goodwin’s own subscription page tells readers that paying supporters finance his team, polling, social-media outreach and short films. His newsletter has advertised an audience exceeding 90,000 readers. 

We can therefore describe the observable mechanism:

\text{attention}
\rightarrow
\text{audience}
\rightarrow
\text{subscribers}
\rightarrow
\text{revenue}.

Now consider which headline is likely to compete more successfully for attention.

A:

Some potentially interesting but geographically limited results from a Kent council by-election

or:

B:

WHY THIS SHOULD TERRIFY THE RIGHT

This is not uniquely a Goodwin problem.

It is the political creator economy.

Anger travels.

Fear travels.

Vindication travels.

“It’s complicated and I need another six months of data” struggles somewhat on TikTok.

Hence the Rage Yield Function:

R \propto U \times O \times C

where:

U = urgency,

O = outrage,

and C = certainty.

Nuance is represented by N, which unfortunately appears in the denominator.

Engagement
\approx
\frac{UOC}{N}

Peer review remains ongoing.

