9. Policy: The Mysteriously Empty Part of the Rage Machine

Political anger is easy.

Government is fucking difficult.

Suppose we accept the premise:

Britain is being destroyed.

Fine.

Now comes the boring bit.

How many nurses?

How many police officers?

What tax rate?

What borrowing requirement?

What planning legislation?

Which energy mix?

What grid investment?

What social-care settlement?

What productivity programme?

How many houses?

Where?

At what density?

Who pays?

Which department administers it?

What legislation is required?

How long does implementation take?

What happens when Policy A collides with Policy B?

Welcome to governing.

The difference between an angry commentator and someone capable of administering a state begins approximately here.

A country is not managed by identifying enemies at increasingly impressive font sizes.

