10. The Scholar’s Paradox

And this is why Goodwin is such a fascinating subject.

He should know all this.

He has studied populism.

He knows about grievance formation.

He knows about in-groups and out-groups.

He knows political identity is constructed.

He knows media incentives matter.

He knows how declining institutional trust can be converted into political mobilisation.

He knows the phrase “the elite” can function not merely as description but as narrative technology.

And yet his contemporary output repeatedly employs precisely these mechanisms.

His January 2026 writing described an approaching historic turning point, accelerating change, decisions made without consent and politicians refusing honesty. His CPAC speech later told audiences “We’re running out of time.” 

There is something almost beautiful about it.

Jane Goodall studied chimpanzees.

Matt Goodwin studied populists.

Only one eventually started doing the act.

