11. Discussion: The Politics of Permanent Fucking Emergency

The deeper problem is not Matt Goodwin.

There will always be Matt Goodwins.

The problem is an information market increasingly capable of rewarding people for convincing audiences that every morning represents approximately the final forty-five minutes of Western civilisation.

Britain has problems.

Serious ones.

Housing.

Productivity.

Migration.

Energy.

Regional inequality.

State capacity.

Healthcare.

Infrastructure.

Social cohesion.

Those subjects deserve serious argument precisely because they matter.

Turning every one of them into another instalment of:

YOU HAVE BEEN BETRAYED AND THEY THINK YOU’RE STUPID

does not make the analysis braver.

It makes the analysis easier.

Real policy requires uncertainty.

Trade-offs.

Numbers.

Opportunity costs.

Administrative capability.

Occasionally discovering your preferred explanation is wrong.

Permanent outrage has a wonderful advantage over governing:

it never has to work.

It merely has to post again tomorrow.

⸻

12. Conclusion

Matt Goodwin’s contemporary public output presents an unusually useful case study in the transformation of political analysis into political content.

The former academic has become campaigner, candidate, broadcaster, subscription writer and political advocate. His work frequently uses a vocabulary of emergency, betrayal, destruction, hidden truth and impending catastrophe while inviting an audience to finance continued production and distribution of that work. 

None of this proves that his substantive political preferences are wrong.

Those propositions must be tested individually.

But it does establish why the presentation deserves scrutiny.

Because there is a fundamental difference between:

Britain has problems that require argument

and:

BRITAIN IS BEING DESTROYED

The first sentence requires a policy programme.

The second requires Wi-Fi.

And perhaps the strangest feature of Matt Goodwin’s career is that a man who once professionally examined how grievance, identity, distrust and political alienation can be organised into populist movements now sits inside the machine pulling precisely those levers.

Perhaps he has not abandoned his research after all.

Perhaps this is participant observation.

Very, very participant observation.
